NNAAMMEE
       chrt - manipulate the real-time attributes of a process

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       cchhrrtt [options] _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y _c_o_m_m_a_n_d _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t ...

       cchhrrtt [options] --pp [_p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y] _P_I_D

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       cchhrrtt sets or retrieves the real-time scheduling attributes of an
       existing _P_I_D, or runs _c_o_m_m_a_n_d with the given attributes.

PPOOLLIICCIIEESS
       --oo, ----ootthheerr
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__OOTTHHEERR (time-sharing scheduling).
           This is the default Linux scheduling policy.

       --ff, ----ffiiffoo
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO (first in-first out).

       --rr, ----rrrr
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR (round-robin scheduling). When no
           policy is defined, the SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR is used as the default.

       --bb, ----bbaattcchh
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__BBAATTCCHH (scheduling batch processes).
           Linux-specific, supported since 2.6.16. The priority argument has
           to be set to zero.

       --ii, ----iiddllee
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__IIDDLLEE (scheduling very low priority
           jobs). Linux-specific, supported since 2.6.23. The priority
           argument has to be set to zero.

       --dd, ----ddeeaaddlliinnee
           Set scheduling policy to SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE (sporadic task model
           deadline scheduling). Linux-specific, supported since 3.14. The
           priority argument has to be set to zero. See also ----sscchheedd--rruunnttiimmee,
           ----sscchheedd--ddeeaaddlliinnee and ----sscchheedd--ppeerriioodd. The relation between the
           options required by the kernel is runtime ⇐ deadline ⇐ period. cchhrrtt
           copies _p_e_r_i_o_d to _d_e_a_d_l_i_n_e if ----sscchheedd--ddeeaaddlliinnee is not specified and
           _d_e_a_d_l_i_n_e to _r_u_n_t_i_m_e if ----sscchheedd--rruunnttiimmee is not specified. It means
           that at least ----sscchheedd--ppeerriioodd has to be specified. See sscchheedd(7) for
           more details.

SSCCHHEEDDUULLIINNGG OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --TT, ----sscchheedd--rruunnttiimmee _n_a_n_o_s_e_c_o_n_d_s
           Specifies runtime parameter for SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE policy
           (Linux-specific).

       --PP, ----sscchheedd--ppeerriioodd _n_a_n_o_s_e_c_o_n_d_s
           Specifies period parameter for SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE policy
           (Linux-specific).

       --DD, ----sscchheedd--ddeeaaddlliinnee _n_a_n_o_s_e_c_o_n_d_s
           Specifies deadline parameter for SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE policy
           (Linux-specific).

       --RR, ----rreesseett--oonn--ffoorrkk
           Use SSCCHHEEDD__RREESSEETT__OONN__FFOORRKK or SSCCHHEEDD__FFLLAAGG__RREESSEETT__OONN__FFOORRKK flag.
           Linux-specific, supported since 2.6.31.

       Each thread has a _r_e_s_e_t_-_o_n_-_f_o_r_k scheduling flag. When this flag is set,
       children created by ffoorrkk(2) do not inherit privileged scheduling
       policies. After the _r_e_s_e_t_-_o_n_-_f_o_r_k flag has been enabled, it can be
       reset only if the thread has the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__NNIICCEE capability. This flag is
       disabled in child processes created by ffoorrkk(2).

       More precisely, if the _r_e_s_e_t_-_o_n_-_f_o_r_k flag is set, the following rules
       apply for subsequently created children:

       •   If the calling thread has a scheduling policy of SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO or
           SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR, the policy is reset to SSCCHHEEDD__OOTTHHEERR in child processes.

       •   If the calling process has a negative nice value, the nice value is
           reset to zero in child processes.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa, ----aallll--ttaasskkss
           Set or retrieve the scheduling attributes of all the tasks
           (threads) for a given PID.

       --mm, ----mmaaxx
           Show minimum and maximum valid priorities, then exit.

       --pp, ----ppiidd
           Operate on an existing PID and do not launch a new task.

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Show status information.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

UUSSAAGGEE
       The default behavior is to run a new command:
           cchhrrtt _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s]

       You can also retrieve the real-time attributes of an existing task:
           cchhrrtt --pp _P_I_D

       Or set them:
           cchhrrtt --rr --pp _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y _P_I_D

PPEERRMMIISSSSIIOONNSS
       A user must possess CCAAPP__SSYYSS__NNIICCEE to change the scheduling attributes of
       a process. Any user can retrieve the scheduling information.

NNOOTTEESS
       Only SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO, SSCCHHEEDD__OOTTHHEERR and SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR are part of POSIX 1003.1b
       Process Scheduling. The other scheduling attributes may be ignored on
       some systems.

       Linux' default scheduling policy is SSCCHHEEDD__OOTTHHEERR.

